00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
this week's classic interview is very  much related to the upcoming election  and it's very very much related to an  article I read earlier this week I think  I mentioned this on the Monday show  there was an article I believe in New  York magazine which argued that it's  really great that Hillary Clinton can go  into much more political depth than  Donald Trump but her wonkiness may not  serve her that well because we have such  a politicized status quo in this country  which is very very attractive to many  people that Hillary Clinton being able  to go into depth on issues may not  actually be that appealing to many many  voters and that's exactly what I  discussed with Peter Coleman back in  January of 2014 this interview was very  relevant then given the struction ISM  political stagnation that was existing  between House Republicans and President  Obama at the time and it is very  relevant in sort of understanding what  we're seeing now we're in in a normal  world in an ideal world I don't even  know what adjectives to assign to it at  this point in a world that made more  sense to me any reasonable person would  see Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton and  say this is not a question no matter my  political beliefs Donald Trump is just  fundamentally not qualified to be  President of the United States but  because there is thus attraction to the  politicized and polarized status quo of  our country this this duopoly that we  have uh someone like Donald Trump  actually could be President of the  United States so let's get to my  interview from 2014 with Peter Coleman  i'm joined today by Professor Peter  Coleman he's professor of psychology and  education at Columbia University also  director of the international center for  cooperation and conflict resolution and  author of the five percent finding  solutions to seemingly impossible  conflicts let's start professor maybe  with that title who are the five percent  that you describe the five percent comes  from some research that looks at really  international conflicts over about a  200-year period and what researchers  have found is that if you look at  all of the conflicts that have taken  place really between states and nations  in that time a very small percentage of  them about 5% of them get stuck into  kind of an ongoing very polarized  destructive dynamic that sometimes lasts  40 50 60 years or more and they are  unresponsive to typical attempts to  resolve them diplomacy mediation  negotiations they there's some evidence  that those types of strategies you  actually make them worse so it's those  five percent of conflicts that we study  in the international domain but we also  see evidence of these types of dynamics  that take place in the in domestic  relations and political dynamics here in  the pro-life pro-choice conflict in some  communities etc so okay so that that  gives us a sense so I I guess you kind  of started to answer what was going to  be my follow-up which is speaking  domestically you talked about pro-choice  pro-life what other issues would kind of  fall within these seemingly intractable  conflicts domestically well I mean they  can happen in families often times you  know there is a dispute between siblings  between parents and children that that  become protracted and really people feel  constrained by and really see no way out  there are instances in new york city  where I live where you'll have a couple  that will need to divorce but stay in  the same apartment because it can't  afford another dwelling so they get  stuck into this kind of negative dynamic  and they're trapped because they can't  physically leave so those are the basic  conditions and they happen again in  families that happen in communities they  happen in you know universities they  happen at our university where you'll  see an ongoing dispute columbia  university where i teach has had an  ongoing dispute organized around the  israeli-palestinian intentions but on  campus where Israeli students or Jewish  students feel discriminated against by  certain professors and and so those  types of dynamics can play out  domestically as well as in turn  thinking about so-called kind of  partisanship in the context of American  politics since the Civil War have there  been changes in the amount of so-called  bipartisanship or the two sides that the  Democrats and Republicans working  together over time or has it been  roughly the same ya know there has been  considerable changes there again there's  data on this over several generations a  couple of centuries going back to before  the u.s. Civil War and the data looks at  really whether Republicans Democrats  will cross the aisle and support a  bipartisan bill or support a bill  proposed by the other side and what we  found is that politics today in the  United States in terms of voting  behaviors of Republicans and Democrats  are more polarized and they have been  since the u.s Civil War so we've become  increasingly more polarized in our  inability to have the politicians sort  of come together on consensus on  solutions to issues and that the the  current trajectory the current trend  towards more polarization started around  nineteen eighty that's when we really  started to see more and more  polarization but yes today it's it's it  um the voting patterns are more  polarizing then really ever before in  history certainly back as far as the UH  the Civil War one could make any number  of analyses about why that might be and  I want to throw a couple of ideas at you  and see if you think either neither or  both have some validity some people  email me and they say the reason why we  don't get anywhere is it politically  between Democrats and Republicans is  because there are only Democrats and  Republicans there is this two parties  duopoly status quo and that the  injection of some viable third-party  would shake up this it's either one or  the other kind of alternatives in force  pull up politicians to to work together  to create more alliances there's another  side all together which emails me and  says it's not so much about a third  party but it's about  it's kind of like a game theory  discussion where there's almost no  advantage to elected officials to be  bipartisan because they've got their  next election to think of they raise  money in in great part because of  because of these partisan issues that  you talked about are either of these  have kind of evaluate the either or both  of those ideas yeah I think they're both  valid issues there is an incentive  structure that keeps politicians focused  more on short-term gains and being  reelected or moving into ideological  beliefs and seeing compromise as the  enemy there are there are in fact many  good arguments for why we are as stuck  as we are what we believe is that it's  really some combination of all of those  things that creates basically a set of  constraints on politicians that really  trap them and force them to sort of  behave in a certain way that's  consistent with the you know the  structure of the system so what does  that mean that means that even though  politics in this country could be the  polarization of politics could be traced  back to things like gerrymandering to  money in politics the impact of lobbying  to religious beliefs or two more  fanaticism you know to sort of the  extremes both parties driving the  parties all of those things are valid  arguments but what we believe is that  somehow these things have sort of come  together and support each other in  complex ways and so that even when one  of these strategies one of these issues  one of these drivers of the polarization  drops away or is addressed or somehow is  you know effectively remedied it doesn't  seem to have an impact on the on the  patterns the patterns still stay stuck  and that's because they're multiple  terminals why they are stuck in this way  and a lot of the typical solutions that  people try to introduce don't seem to  have much impact I know that at the  State of the Union not this year but a  couple of years back they congressman  decided to sit not across the aisle from  each other for the student union  sit next to each other right there was a  lot of ridicule regarding that strategy  but what that reflects is the fact that  there is this change in the culture of  Washington where it used to be that  politicians would move their families to  DC and their families would go to school  together and their kids would hang out  together and they would have other kinds  of contact other than the sort of  disputes that they would face daily and  that allowed them that created a  different kind of dynamic between them  that was more reasonable and more  conciliatory and that's changed and so  this attempt to sit next to each other  during the State of the Union was some  attempt to begin to reconstruct how  these political actors interface but let  me just say one other thing if I can  because it's easy to see the divisions  in DC because we can count votes we can  see the political rhetoric coming  particularly from the extremes of both  sides but this is reflective of our  country of where we are in our personal  lives most of us in in the media that we  watch in the opinions that we hold in  the parties that we go to in the really  more exclusionary nature of our social  dynamics this is really a bottom-up  process and the population of this  country really need to take as much  responsibility I believe as our  leadership very quickly one other thing  I want to just kind of throw in is when  you talk about the bottom-up approach do  people have a desire to find simple  explanations for political issues in one  example I'm drawn to is the language  around tax relief is something that has  been very effective for the american  right in the sense that hey if lower  taxes are relief that's very simple more  taxes are bad less taxes are good when  the reality is a very nuanced Shades of  Grey argument that is neither more  videos net neither a ton nor is it no  taxes but people seem driven to simple  explanations yeah that's absolutely true  i think under difficult circumstances  difficult economic times under  threatening times such as post 9-11  people are anxious and they do want to  have a sense of sort of simple solutions  provided by their leaders but  by governments and they latch onto those  and so you know political operatives  understand this they understand that if  you frame something that resonates with  people's fears concerns basic needs that  they'll latch on to it and they'll  support it even if it's only half  correct but there are other  organizations in this country that sort  of try to counteract that I mean the  media to some degree some media to some  degree will try to provide more of a  balanced perspective on these issues but  there are also dialogue forums such as  the national issues forum which I would  recommend your viewers go to they have a  website and they try to present the  nuance of a lot of these more  complicated issues the this sort of gray  area and say that it you know we're not  we're not trapped by the polarized  dynamic that there are there are  solutions there are strategies that that  are more informed by the complexity of  these problems we've been speaking with  Professor Peter Coleman professor of  psychology and education at Columbia  University check out the five percent  finding solutions to seemingly  impossible conflicts really a pleasure  having you on thank you David thank you  very much

